    "body": "`TestCPUAdamGPUError` and `TestCPUAdagradGPUError` assert that the CPU optimizer rejects a param living on a discrete accelerator:\n\n```python\ndevice = get_accelerator().device_name(0)  # 'cuda:0' or 'xpu:0'\nparam = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.randn(model_size, device=device))\n...\nwith pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n    optimizer.step()\n```\n\nOn the cpu accelerator the param is already on cpu, so the assert cannot fire — and the test never returns. In the first `cpu-torch-multi-latest` nightly (issue #8834, run https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/actions/runs/38849719748) two of the four xdist workers were stuck on exactly these two tests: they were the only items of the 851-test phase that started and never reported a result, and the run sat at 93-94% until the 120m phase budget killed it.\n\nThis marks both tests with an item-level `skipif` so they are skipped before `tests/unit/common.py` launches the rank pool, matching the guard `TestCPULionGPUError` already has:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.skipif(get_accelerator().device_name() == 'cpu', reason=\"requires a discrete accelerator device\")\n```\n\nVerified on a CPU-only machine (8 vCPU), with `DS_MULTIRANK_ONLY=1 LOCAL_SIZE=4`: both tests skip in ~2s without launching a rank pool, where the unmarked versions leave pytest wedged.\n\nThe shared test harness has related robustness gaps (an unbounded `pool.starmap`/`join` in `DistributedTest._close_pool`, one master port handed to every pool, and `/dev/shm` allreduce segments the op never unlinks). Those are deliberately left out of this PR; it only stops the nightly from selecting tests that cannot pass on cpu.",
    "changed_files": 2,
    "closed_at": null,
